>> title: Irish Stew, Slow Cooker
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: lamb, potatoes, irish, one-pot, stew, slow cooker, crock pot
>> counters: The Slow Cooker
>> dish: irish-stew
>> kit: Slow Cooker
>> aka: lamb stew, Irish lamb stew, slow cooker irish stew, crockpot lamb stew
>> pairs-with: no-knead-bread
>> servings: 6
>> time: 8 hr 15 min
>> slack: forgiving — everything in this pot is a root or a shoulder and none of it has a window; the barley is the only thing that changes with the hours, and all it does is thicken
>> step.2: season the lamb
>> step.3: layer it all in cold
>> step.4: slow cook on low, 8 hr
>> step.5: finish with parsley and pepper

Written for low, eight hours, and this is the easiest file on the shelf: nothing is browned, nothing goes in late, and there is no pan to wash. That is not a shortcut — a traditional Irish stew is a pale stew. The lamb goes in raw and the onions go in raw, and the broth is meant to come out white rather than brown. Every other file here either browns in a skillet or explains itself; this one genuinely has nothing to brown. Stock halves to two cups, because nothing leaves the pot all day.

Season @lamb shoulder{3%lb}(1.4 kg; cut in 2-in pieces) with @kosher salt{2%tsp}.

Layer @yellow onions{2%large}(sliced), @carrots{3%large}(cut in 2-in lengths), @pearl barley{1/4%cup}(50 g), @floury potatoes{2%lb}(900 g; Russets, quartered) over @&(~1)seasoned lamb{} in a #slow cooker{}, potatoes on top where they steam rather than sit in the liquid.

Slow cook @&(~1)pot{} with @lamb stock{2%cup}(475 mL; or water), @thyme{4%sprigs}, @bay leaf{1} on low for ~slow cook{8%hr}, lid on.

Finish @&(~1)stew{} with @flat-leaf parsley{2%Tbs}(chopped), @black pepper{1%tsp}, crushing two or three of the potatoes into the broth with the back of a spoon, which is all the thickening it wants.
